  "description": {
    "ja": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 日本語版「カムカム」",
      "source_url": "https://ja.wikipedia.org/wiki/%E3%82%AB%E3%83%A0%E3%82%AB%E3%83%A0",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 106353292,
      "text": "カムカム はフトモモ科の常緑低木。ペルーのアマゾン川流域の熱帯雨林に産し、Camu camu、CamuCamu、Cacari、Camocamoなどの名で呼ばれる。近縁な植物にはジャボチカバや、グアバベリーがある。"
    },
    "en": {
      "source": "Wikipedia 英語版「Myrciaria dubia」",
      "source_url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Myrciaria_dubia",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",
      "retrieved": "2026-10-09",
      "revision": 1360717401,
      "text": "Myrciaria dubia, commonly known as camu-camu, caçari, araçá-d'água, or camocamo, is a species of plant in the family Myrtaceae. It is a small bushy riverside tree from the Amazon rainforest in Peru and Brazil, which grows to a height of 3–5 metres and bears a red/purple cherry-like fruit. It is a close relative of the false jaboticaba and the guavaberry or rumberry. As much as 2–3% of the fresh fruit by weight is vitamin C."
    }
